        <!-- SECTION 1: Cool-Season vs Warm-Season: The Only Decision That Really Matters -->
        <div id="cool-vs-warm-season" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Cool-Season vs Warm-Season: The Only Decision That Really Matters
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before comparing a single brand, you need to know which of two biological categories your climate supports. Getting this wrong guarantees failure regardless of how much you spend or how carefully you water, and it is the reason most bags of grass seed that die were doomed the moment they were purchased.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cool-Season Grasses</h3>

          <p>
            Tall fescue, Kentucky bluegrass, perennial ryegrass, and fine fescue grow most actively when air temperatures sit roughly between 60 and 75 degrees Fahrenheit. That means two growth surges, one in spring and a stronger one in fall, with a summer slowdown where the grass may go dormant and brown in heat. They dominate the northern half of the country, roughly from the Mid-Atlantic and Midwest northward, and they are seeded in late summer to early fall for best results.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Warm-Season Grasses</h3>

          <p>
            Bermudagrass, zoysia, centipede, and St. Augustine grow most actively between roughly 80 and 95 degrees Fahrenheit. They thrive through a Southern summer that kills fescue, and they go completely dormant and straw-brown after the first hard frost. They are seeded or sodded in late spring through early summer once soil has genuinely warmed, and they dominate the South and Southwest.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Transition Zone</h3>

          <p>
            A band across the middle of the country, running roughly through Virginia, Tennessee, Arkansas, Oklahoma, and Kansas, is hot enough to stress cool-season grasses and cold enough to damage warm-season ones. Turf-type tall fescue is the most common answer here because its deep roots handle summer heat while it stays green through winter, but it is genuinely the hardest region in the country to grow a lawn in and expectations should be adjusted accordingly.
          </p>

