        <!-- SECTION 2: The Grass Types Explained -->
        <div id="grass-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Grass Types Explained
          </h2>

          <p>
            Within each climate category, the individual species differ enormously in drought tolerance, wear resistance, shade tolerance, and whether they can repair themselves. Most consumer bags are blends of several species, and knowing what is in the mix tells you far more than the marketing name on the front.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Turf-Type Tall Fescue</h3>

          <p>
            The most practical cool-season grass for most homeowners. Deep roots give it the best drought and heat tolerance in its category, it handles moderate shade, and it wears well. Its weakness is that it grows in clumps rather than spreading, so damage needs reseeding. Modern turf-type varieties have a much finer blade than the coarse old pasture fescue people remember.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Kentucky Bluegrass</h3>

          <p>
            The best-looking cool-season grass and the only common one that repairs itself by spreading through underground rhizomes. It demands more water and nitrogen than fescue, is slow to germinate, and thins out in shade and summer heat. Ideal for Northern lawns with irrigation.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Perennial Ryegrass</h3>

          <p>
            The fastest to germinate by a wide margin and a strong performer under foot traffic. It does not spread and is less heat tolerant than fescue, so it is usually a blend component rather than a standalone lawn, and it is the standard choice for winter overseeding of dormant Southern lawns.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fine Fescues</h3>

          <p>
            Creeping red, chewings, and hard fescue are the most shade-tolerant and lowest-input cool-season grasses available. They use little water and little fertilizer, and their fine, needle-like blade looks distinctly different from the rest of the category. Poor traffic tolerance keeps them out of play areas.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bermudagrass and Zoysia</h3>

          <p>
            The two main warm-season seeding options. Bermuda is faster to establish, more aggressive, more traffic tolerant, and needs full sun. Zoysia is slower to establish and denser, with meaningfully better shade tolerance and a plusher feel, but it is often planted as plugs or sod rather than seed because seeded varieties are limited.
          </p>

